FILE = "is-halloween-a-nairobi-thing.html"
TITLE = "Is Halloween a Nairobi thing now?"
TAG = "Flavor Decoded"
H1 = "Is Halloween a Nairobi thing now?"
DEK = "Ten years ago it was a hard sell. Tonight there's a kids' party in Gigiri, a rock night in Ngara and a lot of face paint in between. Here's how a borrowed holiday found its way into the city."
META = "Flavor Decoded · 31 Oct 2026 · 4 min read"
LEAD = ("img/nw2-fright.jpg", "Village Market's Halloween Fright Night", "Village Market (poster)")

BODY = [
("p", "Look at Nairobi's plans for tonight. Village Market in Gigiri is running Halloween Fright Night, with a Little Monsters party for kids from 2pm and a Villain party from 6pm. Across town at Sarakasi Dome in Ngara, Rock Riot is putting on its Halloween edition, with Rash Band on stage. Costumes, as the posters keep saying, are encouraged."),
("p", "None of this is Kenyan tradition. So how did 31 October end up on the city's calendar?"),

("h2", "Then", "A hard ", "sell"),
("p", "In 2016, Business Daily reported that Halloween had meant little to Kenyans only a few years earlier, but that hotels, bars, pastry chefs and costume designers were now working hard to get locals to join in, with entrepreneurs in Nairobi competing to throw the scariest party. The same year, the economist and columnist XN Iraki wrote in The Standard about the strangeness of seeing Halloween banners go up in October, and asked why Kenyans were taking on celebrations that weren't their own."),

("h2", "Now", "A party ", "circuit"),
("p", "By 2024 the question had changed. KBC reported that young Kenyans were hosting and attending Halloween costume parties, and Pulse Kenya counted nine Nairobi venues marking what it called the city's unofficial holiday. At SK8City, the best costume was worth up to KSh 20,000."),
("p", "Some of those nights have become fixtures. Rock Riot ran a Halloween edition at Nairobi Street Kitchen in 2024, and it's back this year at Sarakasi Dome, billing itself as an annual Halloween celebration."),

("h2", "Why now", "Blame the ", "timeline"),
("p", "Most of the credit goes to phones. K24 says young Kenyans pick up Halloween through social media, films and pop culture, with TikTok and Instagram doing much of the work: costume ideas, makeup transformations, themed shoots. KBC's 2024 report had its own example, a TikTok of three creators in horror make-up walking through the CBD while passers-by and security guards stared."),
("p", "Business has followed the crowd. Malls, bars, restaurants, makeup artists and costume sellers now build October around it, the same way Business Daily saw them trying to a decade ago. The difference is that people are actually turning up."),

("h2", "Not everyone", "Hard ", "pass"),
("p", "It isn't a city-wide party. Plenty of Kenyans want nothing to do with it. Parents Africa's look at the debate last year found families split between those who see harmless dress-up and those who see the ghosts and demons as spiritually unsafe. K24's take this year is much the same: some people see fun and creativity, others question taking on a foreign celebration at all."),

("h2", "So", "Is it a Nairobi thing ", "now?"),
("p", "For one slice of the city, yes: young, online and out on a Saturday night. For a lot of Nairobi it's still somebody else's holiday. But it now comes with Kenyan DJs, Kenyan rock bands and Kenyan costumes, and every year the list of places to go gets a little longer."),
("p", "And if you want something scary that really is from here, we've got you. Read <a href=\"africas-monsters-decoded.html\">Africa had its own monsters first</a>."),
]

